[url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/static_switching_system/docs/]Browse: Complete Documentation[/url]

[color=#b48cff][size=5][b]RULES OF ENGAGEMENT: PERSISTENCE AND REMOVAL[/b][/size][/color]

[color=#b48cff][b]Do not assume an SSS module can be safely removed from an existing save.[/b][/color]

Instance modules can make persistent changes to world objects. Removing the YAML later does not automatically reverse changes which already happened.

Treat an SSS module like any other mod capable of modifying your save unless its author specifically documents otherwise.

[url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/static_switching_system/docs/concepts/persistence/]Read: Persistence and Once Semantics[/url]
[url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/static_switching_system/docs/compatibility/save-updates-and-persistence/]Read: Updating and Removing Modules[/url]

[color=#b48cff][size=5][b]DAMAGE CONTROL[/b][/size][/color]

Start with the log.

Then check that the module is actually inside OpenMW's active VFS, that its assets are visible there, and that the YAML validates against the shipped schema.

Do not begin firing wildly into your load order until you have identified the target.

[url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/static_switching_system/docs/compatibility/troubleshooting/]Read: Troubleshooting[/url]
[url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/static_switching_system/docs/compatibility/vfs-package-boundaries/]Read: VFS and Package Boundaries[/url]

[color=#b48cff][size=5][b]THE SHORT VERSION[/b][/size][/color]

If you're a player:

[color=#b48cff][b]Install it. Enable it. Forget about it.[/b][/color]

If you're an author:

[color=#b48cff][b]Identify the target. Define the conditions. Issue the order. Let SSS handle the logistics.[/b][/color]

Turns out the answer to “what can it switch?” was [color=#b48cff][b]yes[/b][/color].

[center][color=#b48cff][b]Don't tell me what you value. Show me your openmw.cfg, and I'll tell you what you value.[/b][/color]

God bless Morrowind. God bless OpenMW. And may Azura protect our load orders.[/center]

